#speculation(title: [Mild and Constitutional Energy Constraint as a Continuous Predisposing Spectrum])[
*(Certainty: 0.45.)* A substantial minority of ME/CFS patients report a lifetime of low or constrained energy — "always more tired than expected," an ADHD/autism-associated energetic profile, or a constitutionally low reserve — rather than a clear pre-illness high baseline @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue @Jason2010FatigueTypes. This raises the question of whether mild/constitutional energy constraint represents a distinct predisposing state on a continuous spectrum, or the mild end of the same illness continuum.

*Evidence.* Severity-based clustering within ME/CFS identifies a low-severity subset that functions with a constrained daily energy envelope @Jason2010FatigueTypes; in more-impaired patients the limiting factor is a low daily energy envelope rather than discrete crashes @Brown2013EnergyEnvelope. A childhood-onset chronic disabling fatigue trajectory, defined without post-exertional malaise, is measurable in adolescence (1.9% at age 13) and persists as a low-functioning state @Collin2015childFatigue. In the general population, persistent fatigue without psychiatric comorbidity is a real construct, but its clearest premorbid predictor is *excessive* childhood energy, not low baseline energy @Harvey2009neurasthenia.

*Competing explanation (equally defensible).* The low-baseline premise is partly contradicted by population data showing high premorbid energy predicts later fatigue @Harvey2009neurasthenia. The two are reconcilable if what matters is a low *reserve ceiling* — a constrained energy envelope that both overactivity and constitutional low-baseline interact with — rather than the resting energy level itself. This reconciliation remains an open question, not an established finding.

*Falsifiability.* If prospective measurement showed that children with objectively low baseline energy capacity (e.g., reduced CPET performance) develop ME/CFS at no higher rate than peers with normal capacity, the low-reserve-ceiling predisposing model would be unsupported. Conversely, a demonstrated dose-response between premorbid reserve deficit and post-trigger illness onset would support it.

*Not yet replicated.* No longitudinal cohort has tracked mild/constitutional low-reserve individuals into ME/CFS onset; the transition to moderate/severe disease remains unmeasured. (Severity applicability: mild / lifetime-energy. Moderate-to-severe transition unknown.)

*(Origin: brainstorm.)*

*Consequence:* If mild constitutional energy constraint is a genuine predisposing state, then these patients warrant the same pacing education and PEM screening as anyone — but the label should not be used to withhold the diagnosis, since PEM, not baseline energy, is what distinguishes disease.
] <spec:mild-constitutional-energy-spectrum>
